RoutSurs
========

RoutSurs is an operating-system build framework for embedded and
network-oriented devices.  It cross-compiles a complete Linux system - from
the toolchain up to the root filesystem, the kernel and the bootloader - from
a single configuration file.

RoutSurs is a downstream distribution based on Buildroot.  The build engine,
the package infrastructure and the bulk of the board support are the work of
the Buildroot developers; RoutSurs adds its own name, versioning, branding and
default configurations on top of that.  Upstream copyright notices and the
license in COPYING are kept unchanged - see the NOTICE file for details.

Quick start
-----------

To build and use RoutSurs, do the following:

1) run 'make menuconfig'
2) select the target architecture and the packages you wish to compile
3) run 'make'
4) wait while it compiles
5) find the kernel, bootloader, root filesystem, etc. in output/images

You do not need to be root to build or run RoutSurs.  Have fun!

RoutSurs comes with a basic configuration for a number of boards. Run
'make list-defconfigs' to view the list of provided configurations, or start
from the RoutSurs defaults with:

    make routsurs_qemu_aarch64_defconfig
    make

Documentation
-------------

The documentation lives in docs/manual.  You can generate a text document
with 'make manual-text' and read output/docs/manual/manual.text.

Branding
--------

The name of the produced operating system is defined in one place: the
BRAND_NAME / BRAND_ID / BRAND_VERSION variables at the top of the Makefile.
They are used for the kconfig main menu, /etc/issue, /etc/os-release, the help
text and the release tarball name.  See BRANDING.md for the full list of
user-visible branding points and for the rules about attribution.

